      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense regarding the methodological hurdles. However, when looking at the actual data clusters in large-scale GWAS (Genome-Wide Association Studies), there seem to be recurring patterns in certain non-European groups regarding higher scores in predispositions for aggression or lower levels of additive social cohesion. From a purely evolutionary perspective, what kind of selective environments would theoretically drive those specific phenotypic skews in those lineages? Are there specific papers or theories that attempt to map those behavioral phenotypes back to ancestral subsistence patterns?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
